# MZ Logistics Service Provider Platform - Flutter Rules

Follow the global MZ Logistics rules.

This application serves transportation companies and service providers.

Supports:
- Web
- Android
- iOS

## Core Principle

The Service Provider is the parent business account.

Under the Service Provider account are:

- Company users
- Trucks
- Vehicles
- Equipment
- Drivers
- Quotations
- Jobs
- Trips
- Financial information

Drivers and trucks must never be treated as independent service provider accounts.

## Main Features

Structure features around:

- Dashboard
- Shipment Requests
- Quotations
- Jobs
- Trips
- Dispatch
- Fleet
- Trucks
- Equipment
- Drivers
- Documents
- Finance
- Notifications
- Company Settings
- Users
- Roles & Permissions

## Roles

The UI must respect backend permissions.

Possible roles include:

Provider Admin:
- Full company access.

Operations Manager:
- Jobs.
- Trips.
- Operations.
- Dispatch.

Dispatcher:
- Assign trucks.
- Assign drivers.
- Manage trip scheduling.

Fleet Manager:
- Trucks.
- Vehicles.
- Equipment.
- Driver operational data.

Finance:
- Payments.
- Invoices.
- Settlements.

Do not rely on role name alone when backend provides permissions.

Use permissions to determine what the user can:
- View.
- Create.
- Update.
- Delete.
- Approve.
- Assign.

## Desktop Experience

Provider web experience should be operational and information-dense without becoming cluttered.

Use:
- Tables.
- Filters.
- Search.
- Dashboards.
- Side navigation.
- Status indicators.

Fleet and trip management should be efficient on desktop.

## Mobile Experience

Mobile should focus on important operational actions.

Avoid copying large desktop tables directly to mobile.

Convert data intelligently into:
- Cards.
- Expandable rows.
- Compact summaries.
- Mobile filters.

## Quotations

Quotation forms must clearly support:

- Total price.
- Number of trucks.
- Truck types.
- Truck capacities.
- Number of trips.
- Quantity per trip.
- Execution duration.
- Additional cost or conditions.

## Jobs and Trips

Clearly distinguish between:

Job
and
Trip.

Never visually or logically merge them.

One Job may contain multiple Trips.

## Fleet

Truck information must clearly show:
- Truck type.
- Capacity.
- Availability.
- Assigned driver where applicable.
- Current operational state.

## Validation

Prevent invalid assignments in UI where possible, but backend remains authoritative.

Examples:
- Over-capacity truck.
- Unavailable truck.
- Unavailable driver.
- Invalid trip state.